# Copy this file to .env.local for local development.
# Never commit a real .env.local file.
#
# Everything in this file is either exposed to the browser (NEXT_PUBLIC_*)
# or read only at build/server time. Never put a backend secret here.

# Origin of the SupportPilot AI backend — scheme + host + port, NO path
# suffix. The generated OpenAPI paths (src/types/api.ts) are already
# absolute, including the /api/v1 prefix (e.g. "/api/v1/auth/login/"), so
# apiClient (src/lib/api/client.ts) joins this origin directly with those
# paths — appending /api/v1 here would double it.
#
# The browser calls this directly, so it must be reachable from the client,
# an origin the backend's CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS/CSRF_TRUSTED_ORIGINS allow
# (see backend/config/settings.py) — AND, critically, the exact same
# hostname the frontend itself is served from (see frontend/README.md,
# "Cookie semantics"): this frontend's CSRF flow needs to read a cookie the
# backend sets with no explicit cookie Domain, which only works when both
# share one hostname, not merely a registrable domain/sibling subdomain.
#
# Local dev (this default): frontend http://localhost:3000, backend
# http://localhost:8000 — different origins (different ports), but the
# SAME hostname ("localhost"), which is what makes it work.
NEXT_PUBLIC_API_BASE_URL=http://localhost:8000

# Production illustration ONLY — never put a real domain in committed
# config. Route the backend under the SAME browser-facing hostname as the
# frontend via a path-routing reverse proxy (e.g. an external load balancer
# sending /api/v1/* to the backend and everything else to the frontend),
# and point this at that one hostname:
#   NEXT_PUBLIC_API_BASE_URL=https://supportpilot.example.com
# A separate API hostname (even a sibling subdomain such as
# api.example.com) breaks the CSRF cookie read described above.
